## The shared Google grant (each of these fails quietly if missed)

Google keeps ONE grant per (account, OAuth client), and a revoke ends all of
it. Talos therefore has to know that this connection is Google's, and whose
account it is.

- [ ] `talos-oauth/src/lib.rs`: add `"__SNAKE__"` to
      `GOOGLE_SHARED_CLIENT_PROVIDERS` (left out, the token is never
      refreshed: the connection works for an hour) and to
      `GOOGLE_REVOKE_PROVIDERS`. The two coverage tests beside them list the
      providers by name; add it there too.
- [ ] `talos-oauth/src/credentials.rs`, `active_google_connections`: a
      `WHEN c.provider = '__SNAKE__' THEN (SELECT lower(w.account_email) FROM
      __SNAKE___integrations w WHERE w.user_id = c.user_id AND
      w.provider_key::text = c.provider_key ORDER BY w.updated_at DESC LIMIT
      1)` arm. Without it this connection's account is unknown, and a
      connection that cannot be placed WITHHOLDS the revoke of every other
      Google disconnect the user makes.
- [ ] `controller/tests/google_disconnect_shared_grant_tests.rs`: the insert
      arm for `"__SNAKE__"` and a test like
      `the_calendar_write_connection_shares_its_accounts_grant`.
- [ ] Register the redirect URI (`…/api/__ID__/callback`) on the shared
      Google OAuth client, and add the scope to its consent screen.

